    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across devices. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices within a layer.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models, where different devices hold *different experts* (entire FFN blocks), and tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nIn practice, modern serving stacks (Megatron-style deployments, vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, DeepSpeed-MI, SGLang) combine:\n\n- **Data parallelism (DP)** across replica groups for throughput,\n- **TP** within a node (NVLink domain) for dense layers and attention,\n- **EP** across nodes or within a node for MoE layers,\n- sometimes **pipeline parallelism (PP)** for very deep models.\n\nThis note focuses on the mechanics of TP and EP during *inference* (decode and prefill), the communication patterns, and how to reason about correctness and bottlenecks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The standard Megatron-style split:\n\n- **Attention block**:\n  - QKV projection `W_qkv: [d_model, 3 * d_head * n_heads]` is **column-parallel**: each TP rank owns a contiguous slice of heads (e.g., 32 heads over 8 ranks \u2192 4 heads/rank).\n  - Output projection `W_o: [d_head * n_heads, d_model]` is **row-parallel**: each rank owns a slice of input columns matching its heads.\n- **MLP block**:\n  - First (up/gate) projection is **column-",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
